# gpu-metrics.service — Host-first deployment systemd unit

## Overview

`gpu-metrics.service` is a **10-second sysfs sampler** for AMD GPU metrics. It
is a bash loop that reads the `amdgpu` driver's attributes under
`/sys/class/drm/card0/device/` and writes a Prometheus textfile
(`gpu.prom`) into node_exporter's textfile collector directory, where
[node_exporter.service](node_exporter.service) exposes it on
`127.0.0.1:9100/metrics`.

It exists because node_exporter has **no amdgpu collector** and the AMD
rocm-exporter publishes no release artefacts ([rocm-exporter.service](rocm-exporter.service)).
Reading sysfs needs no root, no ROCm userspace and no extra daemon — the loop
costs one `cat` per attribute every 10 s.

Two things this unit is **not**:

- It is not a utilisation signal. `gpu_busy_percent` is **not sampled at all**
  (retired 2026-11-07, LP-0MW5K8ZDZ007YSIX): on this Strix Halo APU the driver
  attribute is a **two-state busy counter, not a percentage**, whose step sits
  at ~30 W, so it reads 1–10 % below 30 W and 92–99 % above and *inverts*
  across the band that matters (99.2 % at 40–60 W idle-with-display against
  98.7 % at 90–100 W computing). Utilisation is derived from **power draw**
  (`node_hwmon_power_average_watt`, from node_exporter's hwmon collector) — see
  [monitoring/gpu_compute_rules.yaml](../../monitoring/gpu_compute_rules.yaml).
  The decision and its evidence are recorded under
  [Decision: `gpu_busy_percent` is retired](#decision-gpu_busy_percent-is-retired).
- It is not the workload signal either. Slot occupancy — how many llama-server
  slots exist, how many are processing a task, the per-slot 0/1 state, the
  in-flight task count and a last-successful-sample heartbeat — is published by
  the **proxy** on its own `/metrics` endpoint
  (`llama_slots_total`, `llama_slots_busy`, `llama_slot_busy{slot}`,
  `llama_slots_in_flight_tasks`, `llama_slots_sample_timestamp_seconds`), because
  the proxy already owns the only working `/slots` path (child-port discovery,
  coalescing, failure counter). See
  [docs/systemd/llama-proxy.service](llama-proxy.service) for the sample cadence
  (`server.llama_slot_metrics_interval_seconds`, default 10 s).
- It is not a VRAM reporter in any useful sense: the VRAM carve-out is 1 GiB of
  BIOS-reserved memory while models live in **GTT**. GTT is the pool the memory
  alerts watch ([monitoring/gpu_memory_alerts.yaml](../../monitoring/gpu_memory_alerts.yaml)).

## Metric names

| Series | Labels | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `gpu_vram_used_bytes` | `gpu="card0"` | gauge | Used portion of the VRAM carve-out (`mem_info_vram_used`) |
| `gpu_vram_total_bytes` | `gpu="card0"` | gauge | Size of the VRAM carve-out (`mem_info_vram_total`, 1 GiB here) |
| `gpu_gtt_used_bytes` | `gpu="card0"` | gauge | GTT in use (`mem_info_gtt_used`) — the meaningful pool: models map most of their weights here (~36 GiB of a 128 GiB pool in normal operation) |

**Sampling interval: 10 s** (`sleep 10` in the unit's `ExecStart`). Prometheus
scrapes at 15 s, so consecutive scrapes see alternating samples; that is fine
for gauges that change slowly and are averaged in the rules.

The sampler writes a textfile only when it can read at least one of the memory
attributes above: it publishes `gpu_vram_*` when both VRAM attributes are
readable and `gpu_gtt_used_bytes` when the GTT attribute is. When none is
readable it exits **without writing** rather than publishing zeros — a zero
would silently look like an idle GPU.

`gpu_busy_percent` is **no longer published** by this unit (retired
2026-11-07, LP-0MW5K8ZDZ007YSIX). See the decision below.

## Decision: `gpu_busy_percent` is retired

**Status:** retired 2026-11-07. The sampler no longer reads
`/sys/class/drm/card0/device/gpu_busy_percent`, so the series is no longer
published by this unit. Samples already in the Prometheus store remain
queryable until they fall out of the 90 d retention window, but they are
**historical only** — nothing advances the series any more.

**Why, recorded as evidence rather than as a prohibition.** The counter was
published "for continuity only" and no rule or dashboard ever selected it; a
published series with a plausible name and no consumer is an invitation, and
the only thing stopping a future rule was a lint of the rule files. On this
APU the counter cannot answer the question its name suggests: it is a
**two-state detector**, not a percentage, whose step sits at **~30 W**, i.e.
below the idle-with-display band (p50 45 W), so idle-with-context and computing
both read ~99 % and it **inverts** across the band that matters.

| GPU power band | mean busy, kernel 7.0.0-38 (n = 25,463) | mean busy, kernel 7.0.0-34 (n = 28,655) |
|---|---|---|
| 20–30 W | 10.2 % | 7.2 % |
| 30–35 W | 99.4 % | 92.1 % |
| 40–60 W (idle with display) | 99.5 % | 99.2 % |
| 90–100 W (computing) | 99.4 % | 98.7 % |

Capture: **2026-11-06** on kernel **7.0.0-38**, the whole post-boot window
(2026-10-31 14:28 → 2026-11-06 14:28 UTC, 15 s samples), compared with the
preceding five days on kernel 7.0.0-34 using the same recipe; the full banded
table and its PromQL recipe are in
[monitoring/README.md](../../monitoring/README.md#re-measuring-gpu_busy_percent-discrimination)
(LP-0MW5K7WKZ005VJXA). Note the inversion: 40–60 W reads *higher* than
90–100 W, so no threshold inside the working range rescues the counter.

**What to use instead.** GPU activity is the power-derived recording rule
`gpu:computing:bool` (`node_hwmon_power_average_watt{chip_name="amdgpu"} >=
bool 90`); the attributable workload share is
`gpu:computing_workload:bool`. Neither is produced by this unit.

**The guard moved from the rules to the publisher.** `gpu_busy_percent` stays
in `FORBIDDEN_RULE_SERIES`, and the documented sampler script below is now
scanned by the same guard, so re-adding the attribute to the sampler fails
`proxy/tests/test_monitoring_deployment.py` instead of silently republishing a
counter nobody reads.

## The sampler script

**File location**: `~/.local/bin/gpu-metrics-textfile.sh` (executable)

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Emit AMD GPU memory metrics for node_exporter's textfile collector.
# Source: amdgpu sysfs attributes on /sys/class/drm/card0/device. No root needed.
# The driver's busy counter is deliberately NOT sampled: it is a two-state
# counter, not a utilisation measure on this APU (retired 2026-11-07,
# LP-0MW5K8ZDZ007YSIX; see the decision record in the unit document).
set -u
OUT_DIR="${1:-$HOME/.local/share/node_exporter/textfile}"
DEV="${GPU_SYSFS:-/sys/class/drm/card0/device}"
TMP="$OUT_DIR/.gpu.prom.$$"

read_attr() { [ -r "$DEV/$1" ] && cat "$DEV/$1" 2>/dev/null || echo ""; }

vram_used=$(read_attr mem_info_vram_used)
vram_total=$(read_attr mem_info_vram_total)
gtt_used=$(read_attr mem_info_gtt_used)

have_vram=""; [ -n "$vram_used" ] && [ -n "$vram_total" ] && have_vram=1
have_gtt=""; [ -n "$gtt_used" ] && have_gtt=1
if [ -z "$have_vram" ] && [ -z "$have_gtt" ]; then exit 0; fi

{
  if [ -n "$have_vram" ]; then
    echo '# HELP gpu_vram_used_bytes VRAM currently allocated (sysfs mem_info_vram_used).'
    echo '# TYPE gpu_vram_used_bytes gauge'
    echo "gpu_vram_used_bytes{gpu=\"card0\"} $vram_used"
    echo '# HELP gpu_vram_total_bytes VRAM visible to the driver (sysfs mem_info_vram_total).'
    echo '# TYPE gpu_vram_total_bytes gauge'
    echo "gpu_vram_total_bytes{gpu=\"card0\"} $vram_total"
  fi
  if [ -n "$have_gtt" ]; then
    echo '# HELP gpu_gtt_used_bytes GTT (system RAM mapped for the GPU) in use — the meaningful memory pool on this APU.'
    echo '# TYPE gpu_gtt_used_bytes gauge'
    echo "gpu_gtt_used_bytes{gpu=\"card0\"} $gtt_used"
  fi
} > "$TMP" && mv -f "$TMP" "$OUT_DIR/gpu.prom"
```

The write is atomic (`mv` from a `$$`-suffixed temp file), so node_exporter
never reads a half-written file. `OUT_DIR` can be passed as `$1` and the sysfs
root overridden with `GPU_SYSFS`, which is what the repository test harness
uses to exercise it against a fixture directory.

## User Service (recommended)

**File location**: `~/.config/systemd/user/gpu-metrics.service`

```ini
[Unit]
Description=GPU busy/memory sysfs sampler for node_exporter textfile collector
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'while :; do /home/rgardler/.local/bin/gpu-metrics-textfile.sh; sleep 10; done'
Restart=always
RestartSec=5
StandardOutput=append:/home/rgardler/.local/state/node_exporter/logs/gpu-metrics.log
StandardError=append:/home/rgardler/.local/state/node_exporter/logs/gpu-metrics.log

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
```

The unit deliberately runs the loop inside `ExecStart` rather than using a
systemd timer: a `Type=simple` loop keeps one process for the lifetime of the
service, which is simpler to observe (`systemctl status`) than a
`Type=oneshot` timer and avoids missing a sample after a suspend.

## Setup commands (user service)

```bash
# 1. Install the sampler script (copy the block from § The sampler script above)
mkdir -p ~/.local/bin ~/.local/share/node_exporter/textfile
chmod +x ~/.local/bin/gpu-metrics-textfile.sh

# 2. Run it once by hand and check the output before wiring up systemd
~/.local/bin/gpu-metrics-textfile.sh
cat ~/.local/share/node_exporter/textfile/gpu.prom
# expected: gpu_vram_used_bytes, gpu_vram_total_bytes and gpu_gtt_used_bytes,
#           each with gpu="card0" (gpu_busy_percent is retired and not written)

# 3. Install the unit. docs/systemd/*.service are documents that wrap the unit
#    in an `ini` block, so the block is extracted — `cp` would install markdown.
mkdir -p ~/.config/systemd/user
awk '/^```ini$/{f=1;next} /^```$/{f=0} f' \
  docs/systemd/gpu-metrics.service \
  > ~/.config/systemd/user/gpu-metrics.service

# 4. Enable and start
systemctl --user daemon-reload
systemctl --user enable --now gpu-metrics.service

# 5. Verify
systemctl --user status gpu-metrics.service
journalctl --user -u gpu-metrics.service -n 20
```

## Verification

```bash
# The sampler is running and restarting cleanly
systemctl --user is-active gpu-metrics.service
# expected: active

# The textfile is refreshed roughly every 10 s
date +%s; stat -c %Y ~/.local/share/node_exporter/textfile/gpu.prom
# expected: two epochs within 10 s of each other

# The series reach Prometheus through node_exporter
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:9100/metrics | grep -E '^gpu_'
# expected: three series (gpu_gtt_used_bytes, gpu_vram_used_bytes,
#           gpu_vram_total_bytes) — gpu_busy_percent is retired, so it stops
#           appearing after the sampler is restarted on this version
curl -s --get http://127.0.0.1:9090/api/v1/query \
  --data-urlencode 'query=gpu_gtt_used_bytes'
# expected: a single series, job="node-exporter", instance="localhost:9100",
#           gpu="card0", value ≈ 3.66e10 (34 GiB) after models load

# Cross-check against the driver's own numbers
cat /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used
rocm-smi --showmeminfo vram
```

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause / fix |
|---|---|
| `gpu.prom` never appears | none of the memory attributes is readable: `cat /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used`. On a host without an AMD GPU there is nothing to sample and the script exits 0 by design |
| `gpu_busy_percent` disappeared from Prometheus | Expected: the series was retired on 2026-11-07 (LP-0MW5K8ZDZ007YSIX). History up to that instant is still queryable for as long as it falls inside the 90 d retention window; nothing advances the series |
| File appears but `gpu_*` series are missing in Prometheus | The textfile directory in the script's `OUT_DIR` differs from node_exporter's `--collector.textfile.directory`; compare `systemctl --user cat node_exporter.service` with the unit above |
| `gpu.prom` stops updating | `systemctl --user status gpu-metrics.service`; the log is at `~/.local/state/node_exporter/logs/gpu-metrics.log` |
| `gpu_gtt_used_bytes` is far above installed RAM | GTT is capped by the driver/BIOS carve-out and can exceed physical RAM; the alert thresholds assume a 128 GiB pool — update `monitoring/gpu_memory_alerts.yaml` if that changes |

